    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Slip along frictional surfaces may significantly affect the architecture of a fault zone by creating associated opening mode (secondary) structures such as: wing cracks (splays) and horse tail fractures among others. Depending on their geometry, location and distribution, these structures may enhance the local permeability and connectivity in the fault zone. The 2D Displacement Discontinuity Method (DDM) is combined with a complementarity algorithm to model the slip distribution and the quasi-static formation of wing cracks along a simulated fault surface with or without cohesive end zones (CEZs). Here we focus on meter scale faults where mineralogical heterogeneity (common to laboratory samples) and 3D geometry (common to crustal structures) reasonably can be ignored. The model wing cracks propagate along curving paths towards the direction of greatest remote principal compression, σ1, and the remote stress field has a significant effect on the degree of curvature. Wing cracks are arrested at shorter lengths and experience smaller opening displacements in the presence of greater confining stress, σ 2 .Opening of these wing cracks also facilitates sliding along the fault. Therefore the fault and wing cracks interact mechanically. Even under overall compression, the magnitude of the local tensile stress induced by this mechanical interaction may exceed the resolved compressive stress on the fault resulting in mixed mode (opening and sliding) deformations. Openings along these initially closed surfaces may act as conduits to fluid flow parallel to the slip surface. The smeared out stress concentration in the CEZ suggest that multiple wing cracks may open and propagate in one slip event. The kink angles for these wing cracks increase with distance from the fault tip.",
